INT. ARBITRATION ROOM, OLD COURTHOUSE - DAY

Oak panels. A tall window. A radiator that knocks.

JUDGE ABERNATHY, 60s, bifocals on a chain, presides. MR. OKAFOR, 40s, sharp navy suit, and MR. LINDGREN, 40s, charcoal suit, glare across a narrow table. MS. TSUKAMOTO, 30s, the court reporter, hovers over a steno machine. BAILIFF DUFFY, 50s, leans against the door.

JUDGE ABERNATHY
Mr. Okafor, you may proceed.

OKAFOR
Thank you, Your Honor. Mr. Lindgren's client signed on the fourth. He knew what he was signing.

LINDGREN
He did not. He thought it was the lease.

OKAFOR
Then he should have read it.

LINDGREN
He did. Twice. And he still didn't know what he was signing.

JUDGE ABERNATHY
Mr. Lindgren. Mr. Okafor. Please.

OKAFOR
Your Honor, he is stalling because he has no evidence.

LINDGREN
He is shouting because he has no case.

The radiator knocks. Everyone looks at it.

DUFFY
That's been happening since Monday, Judge.

JUDGE ABERNATHY
Thank you, Duffy.

TSUKAMOTO
Your Honor, may I ask counsel to speak one at a time?

JUDGE ABERNATHY
You may. And you shall.

A beat. Okafor pulls a paper from his folder.

OKAFOR
Exhibit nine. A signature, notarized, dated the fourth.

LINDGREN
(reading, then slowly)
That is not my client's signature.

OKAFOR
What?

LINDGREN
That is yours.

Okafor looks at the page. His face drains. The radiator knocks again, softly.

JUDGE ABERNATHY
Mr. Okafor, would you care to explain?

Okafor laughs. Short. Then longer.

OKAFOR
Your Honor, I may have grabbed the wrong folder.
